+# tdd skill asks clarifying questions about seams even after the user says "just build it"
+
+## Summary
+
+The `tdd` skill tells the agent to agree on test seams with the user before writing code. It does this even when the user has already said to go ahead without discussion ("just build it"). The skill's instruction wins over the user's, and the agent stops to ask questions instead of building.
+
+## Steps to reproduce
+
+1. Start a session in a project repository with the `tdd` skill available.
+2. Ask for a feature and say explicitly that the agent should proceed without checking in, e.g. "just build it".
+3. Let the `tdd` skill trigger.
+
+## Expected behavior
+
+The agent goes ahead with reasonable default seams and writes down the assumptions it made (for example in its reply or in a notes file) so the user can review them afterwards.
+
+## Actual behavior
+
+The agent stopped and asked the user three clarifying questions about seams before doing any work, even though the user had already said not to.
+
+## Suggested fix
+
+Make the "agree seams with the user" step conditional. When the user has said to proceed without questions, or isn't available to answer, the skill should tell the agent to:
+
+- pick sensible seams itself,
+- carry on with the TDD loop, and
+- list the seam choices and other assumptions clearly so they can be changed later.
+
+Wording along the lines of: *"If the user has asked you to proceed without questions, choose seams yourself and record your assumptions instead of asking."*
+
+## Environment
+
+- Skill: `tdd`
+- Trigger context: ordinary project repository; user instruction was "just build it"
